@@s001 Properties and counterexamples
@@s002 dato:actions that are fine,no:action that violates,ora:part that repeats forever,ok:what never arrives
@@s003 Two properties from the course. Above, a <b>safety</b> property ("something bad never happens"), below, a <b>liveness</b> property ("something good eventually happens").
@@s004 Safety: it is enough to reach <b>one action</b> that violates it. Here after three actions size is −1: the counterexample is the <b>finite prefix</b> add, remove, remove. What happens afterwards does not matter.
@@s005 Liveness: no finite prefix is enough, because w2 could always write later. The counterexample is an <b>infinite</b> execution in which w1 and w3 alternate forever and w2 stays waiting (the example of the slides, with three writers).
@@s006 ora:t1,due:t2,ok:property respected,no:counterexample
@@s007 Properties and counterexamples
@@s008 Lecture 5 (<i>Testing &amp; Verification</i>), first part. A concurrent program is correct if it respects its <b>specification</b>, that is a set of properties. To test it you must know what shape a <b>counterexample</b> has: an interleaving that violates the property. Goetz, chapter 12.
@@s009 Safety and liveness
@@s010 Safety
@@s011 Liveness
@@s012 intuition
@@s013 "something bad never happens"
@@s014 "something good eventually happens"
@@s015 examples from the slides
@@s016 two traffic lights at a crossing are never green together; the size field of a collection is never &lt; 0
@@s017 the light eventually turns red; it will always be possible, eventually, to add elements to the collection
@@s018 <b>counterexample</b> (the true definition)
@@s019 a <b>finite prefix</b> of an interleaving, ending with the action that violates the property
@@s020 an <b>infinite</b> interleaving in which the good thing never arrives
@@s021 What a counterexample looks like
@@s022 Deadlock: safety or liveness?
@@s023 Deadlock-freedom is a property of <b>safety</b>: a deadlock is a <b>state</b> in which no thread can go on any more, and it is reached by a finite interleaving (the philosophers who all take the right fork first). The absence of starvation ("every thread eventually enters") is instead <b>liveness</b>: the counterexample is an infinite execution in which one thread stays out forever, like w2 above.
@@s024 The sentence to remember
@@s025 "Program testing can be used to show the presence of bugs, but never to show their absence!" (Dijkstra, in the slides). A test that fails shows a counterexample; a test that passes proves nothing. For safety properties a test can find the counterexample, because it is finite. The lecture focuses only on safety.
@@s026 From the interleaving to the test
@@s027 The counter of the slides (three atomic steps per thread)
@@s028 Two threads repeat these steps forever.
@@s029 Sequential interleavings: no lost increment.
@@s030 Both read the same value: one increment is lost. It is the counterexample to the property "after k increments counter is k".
@@s031 testing = looking for the <mark class="no">bad</mark> interleavings
@@s032 The execution is non-deterministic: a test does not choose the interleaving, it can only make the bad ones more likely to happen. How, on the page <a href="l05-test-concorrenti.html">Writing concurrent tests</a>. To explore them all you need <a href="l05-verifica-formale.html">formal verification</a>.
@@s033 Good interleaving, bad interleaving
